index a907003..14c40cd 100644
--- a/docs/design.md
+++ b/docs/design.md
@@ -405,6 +405,7 @@ How the generated SPIR-V expects the runtime to work:
 | LDS | A `Workgroup` uint array sized from LDS_SIZE, rounded up to a power of two that also covers every constant offset (15 of 486 shaders index past LDS_SIZE). Covers `ds_read/write_b32..b128`, `read2/write2` (+`st64`), `u16`/`i16`/`u8`/`i8` reads (also `ds_read_u16_d16`/`_d16_hi` and, since SHADER-85, `ds_read_u8_d16`/`_d16_hi` and `ds_read_i8_d16`/`_d16_hi`: the byte is zero- or sign-extended 8→16 into one half of the destination VGPR and the other half is kept, per the RDNA2 ISA DS opcode descriptions (opcodes 162–165) and as for the u16 d16 reads; GPU-checked with `cs_w32_lds_u8_d16.asm`, byte values 0x80/0xFF/0x7F with a non-zero `offset:`), `b16`/`b8` writes (atomic and/or, so neighbouring lanes don't race; a 16-bit read or write at byte 3 of a dword also reads or writes byte 0 of the next dword, as llc-15 `-mcpu=gfx1030 -mattr=+unaligned-access-mode` emits one `ds_read_u16`/`ds_write_b16 ... offset:3` for an align-1 halfword; until 2026-11-22 the reads extracted 16 bits at bit 24, undefined in SPIR-V, and the writes kept only byte 3; `tests/semantic/test_lds_u16_cross_dword.py`), the add/sub/min/max/and/or/xor atomics with and without return, `wrxchg`, `cmpst` (`OpAtomicCompareExchange` with DATA as comparator and DATA2 as value), and `ds_bpermute_b32` as a subgroup shuffle. GPU-checked with `cs_w32_lds_u8_d16.asm`, byte values 0x80/0xFF/0x7F with a non-zero `offset:`), `b16`/`b8` writes (atomic and/or, so neighbouring lanes don't race), the add/sub/min/max/and/or/xor atomics with and without return, `wrxchg`, `cmpst` (`OpAtomicCompareExchange` with DATA as comparator and DATA2 as value), and `ds_bpermute_b32` as a subgroup shuffle. `ds_permute_b32`, its forward (scatter) form, has no Vulkan equivalent: each source lane that is on in EXEC publishes `((vAddr + offset) >> 2) mod 32` and each destination lane scans the 32 sources of its half and keeps the last one that targets it (a shuffle of target and data per source lane; under `--wave64-emulate` two buffer rounds and a Function variable instead). Per the ISA pseudocode (`tmp[0..63] = 0`; `next if !EXEC[i]` on the source; `VGPR[VDST][i] = tmp[i]` only `if EXEC[i]`): a destination lane nobody targets gets 0, an off source sends nothing, an off destination keeps its VDST, and the half rule above applies as for `ds_bpermute_b32`. When several sources target one lane the ISA leaves the winner unpredictable ("the value from one of the writers"; its pseudocode says the highest-numbered); the translator takes the highest, and nothing may depend on it. Confirmed by the GPU tests `cs_w32_permute.asm` and, per half of a wave64, `cs_w64x_permute.asm` (SHADER-90: rotation, offset, unwritten lane = 0, off destination keeps its value, off source does not win) and `tests/semantic/test_ds_permute.py`. **Hardware-verified on RDNA2 (`gfx1036`, AMD Ryzen 7 9800X3D iGPU, Linux AMDGPU DRM via `tools/hw_oracle`, wave32 and wave64, SHADER-133)** with `tools/ds_permute_probe.py` (9/9 cases exact in both wave sizes): unaddressed active lanes get 0, off destinations keep VDST, off sources send nothing, 2-/4-/32-way collisions deterministically take the highest-numbered active lane in the 32-lane half (matching the translator), `vAddr + offset` carries from the low 2 bits before `>> 2` mod 32, wave64 runs as two independent 32-lane halves (`+32` wraps within each half), and missing lanes of a partial wave (20 threads in wave32, 40 in wave64) send nothing. A source lane that is off in EXEC reads 0 and an off destination lane keeps its value (RDNA ISA §10.4.4), in wave32 as in wave64. In wave64 the permute also runs as two wave32s: each half reads lanes of its own half. Until 2026-11-21 lanes 32–63 of a 64-wide subgroup read the low half and inactive sources kept their value (`cs_w64x_bpermute`, AMD Radeon 880M); the wave32 path shuffled the raw value until the same day (`cs_bpermute32`: wave32, EXEC without lanes 8–15, so lane 6 reads 0; GPU-checked on RTX 5070 and Radeon 880M). A source lane that doesn't exist, in the missing lanes of a partial wave, also reads 0: the shuffle result is kept only where a ballot of the running invocations has the source lane, since SPIR-V leaves a shuffle from an invocation that isn't running undefined. Before this, RADV returned stale register values that changed between runs (`cs_w64x_partial`, a 40-thread group, native wave64 on AMD RX 6700 XT, RADV Mesa 26.3.1). Writes and atomics run only in active lanes. Indices wrap at the array size, where hardware would return 0 for out-of-range reads. `s_barrier` = `OpControlBarrier` Workgroup/Workgroup, AcquireRelease + WorkgroupMemory. |
 | LDS access order within a wave (`Volatile`) | RDNA2 runs a wave in lockstep with an in-order LDS queue, so a `ds_read` issued after a `ds_write` sees every lane's write with no `s_barrier` between them (`cs_w64x_lds_raw`), and a `ds_write` issued after a `ds_read` does not change what that read returned (`cs_w64x_lds_war`). Translated, a lane is an invocation, and the Vulkan memory model orders nothing between invocations without a barrier or an atomic, so a driver's compiler may move an invocation's load ahead of its own earlier store to another address, or merge two loads of one address. Results consistent with these optimizations were observed with AMD's Windows driver 24.30.46 (LLPC, Vulkan 1.3.302) on a Radeon 880M (RDNA3.5, native 64-wide compute subgroups): on `main` `561b65a8` both tests failed on every run with uninitialized Workgroup memory in all eight words, and in `cs_w64x_lds_war` the second read returned the same words as the first. Rule: every plain LDS `OpLoad` and `OpStore` (`lds_load`, `lds_store` and the two ADDTID forms) carries the `Volatile` memory operand, which prevents elimination, duplication or combination of the access ([SPIR-V memory operands](https://registry.khronos.org/SPIR-V/specs/unified1/SPIRV.html#Memory_Access)). This rule applies to plain accesses only. On that GPU and driver `Volatile` on the store alone or on the load alone made `cs_w64x_lds_raw` return its expected words (hand-edited SPIR-V, 3 of 3 runs each, against 0 of 4 unmodified), and with the rule both tests pass: `run_gpu_tests.py` goes from 210 to 212 of 232 passed with none failing (20 skipped), native and with `LEONIDA_W64_EMULATE=1`. An RTX 5070 Laptop (NVIDIA 621.01) gives the same results before and after (183 of 232 native, 214 emulated; `ms_wave_info_s3` fails both times). The operand changes 17 of the 238 test shaders and nothing but those loads and stores. A [full Xbox 1.0.2.1 corpus comparison](https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/pull/6714#issuecomment-7107280378) translated and validated all 26,503 shaders at both 561b65a8 and 58deee34. Only the added operands differed in the 484 compute and 5 mesh modules that changed. The RAW and WAR results establish a workaround on the tested driver. Portable ordering between invocations requires synchronization appropriate to the accesses (see #2345). Under `--wave64-emulate` the halves meet at the round barriers as before; the operand is emitted there too. CPU-checked by `tests/semantic/test_lds_volatile.py` (every plain access form on the wave32, wave64 and emulated paths, and no other access is Volatile). |
 | `ds_append`, `ds_consume` (LDS) | One LDS add (append) or subtract (consume) of the number of lanes on in EXEC, for the whole wave, at the byte address in the offset field; every active lane gets the value before the op, inactive lanes keep vD (`Gen.ds_append`). Measured natively on RDNA2 `gfx1036` (Radeon iGPU of a Ryzen 9 9950X3D, driver 26.10.1, `tools/hw_oracle` raw kernels, wave32 and wave64 with EXEC in both halves): EXEC 0xf0 appends 4 and returns the old value to lanes 4-7 only; lanes 0, 32 and 63 of a wave64 append 3; M0 (0x4, 0x100, 0x10000, 0x40000, 0x80000) does not move the address, the offset alone is it (RDNA1 not measured). The add is done once, by the first active lane (the wave's EXEC gathered as for `s_bcnt1`); the old value reaches the other lanes by a subgroup shuffle from that lane, or an exchange round under `--wave64-emulate`; a wave64 on 32-wide subgroups without emulation is right only in the half of the first active lane (the known cross-half approximation). The `gds` form stays `Unsupported`. **Until 2026-11-22 both raised `Unsupported`.** GPU-checked with `runtime/test/shaders/cs_w32_ds_append.asm` and `cs_w64_ds_append.asm` (words derived in their headers) on that Radeon (wave64 on a native 64-wide subgroup) and an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080 (driver 616.92; wave64 with `LEONIDA_W64_EMULATE=1`); done by every active lane instead, both fail (the first append leaves 164 instead of 108). `tests/semantic/test_ds_append.py` checks the count, the performing lane and the returned values (its interpreter keeps one LDS copy per invocation). |
+| Wave64 scheduling for LDS append and consume | `ds_append` and `ds_consume` always require a wave exchange in emulated compute shaders. The structured scheduler must keep them out of sync-free multi-wave branches. The synthetic barrier-classification test covers both opcodes in `test_wave64_emulate_opt.py`. |
 | `ds_cmpst_b32` | The non-returning compare-and-store: `M = (M == data0) ? data1 : M`, the same `OpAtomicCompareExchange` as `ds_cmpst_rtn_b32` with the old value dropped (operands vaddr, data0, data1). Source: RDNA2 ISA DS_CMPST_B32. CPU-checked by `tests/semantic/test_lds_atomics_values.py`; GPU-checked by `runtime/test/shaders/cs_lds_b64_cmpst.asm` (hit and miss on an RTX 3070, driver 621.01, Windows 11), and measured on native gfx1100 through `tools/hw_oracle` (hit, miss and hit-to-zero), where GFX11 spells it `ds_cmpstore_b32` with the store value before the compare value. **Not GPU-verified.** GPU-checked by `runtime/test/shaders/cs_lds_b64_cmpst.asm` (hit and miss on an RTX 3070, driver 621.01, Windows 11). |
 | `ds_cmpst_f32`/`_rtn_f32`, `global_atomic_fcmpswap`, `buffer_atomic_fcmpswap` | `tmp = M; M = (tmp == CMP as binary32) ? DATA : tmp`, and the returning forms give `tmp` (RDNA2 ISA). The compare is a float compare (`OpFOrdEqual`): −0 matches +0, and a NaN never matches, not even the same bits. `OpAtomicCompareExchange` compares bits, so this is a loop (`Gen.float_cmpswap_loop`): read M, pick DATA or M by the float compare, and swap that in if M still holds what was read. Exact under contention. Operands: `ds_cmpst_f32` takes CMP then DATA, as `ds_cmpst_b32`; the global and buffer forms take `vdata` as {DATA, CMP}, as `cmpswap`. Inactive lanes, bounds and `glc` are as for their integer siblings. **Not measured:** subnormal inputs; the interpreter compares them exactly. `_x2` forms stay `Unsupported`; the image form is #2653's. Until 2026-11-22 all four raised `Unsupported`. CPU-checked by `tests/semantic/test_float_cmpswap.py` (±0, NaN, ∞, a subnormal, no `glc`, buffer bounds, 32 lanes on one dword). **Not GPU-verified.** |
 | LDS ADDTID (`ds_read_addtid_b32`, `ds_write_addtid_b32`) | The LDS forms address one dword at `M0[15:0] + offset + 4 * lane`, where `offset` is an unsigned 16-bit byte offset and `lane` is the invocation index within the RDNA wave. The Workgroup array supplies LDS_BASE. The compute/mesh path uses the ordinary LDS addressing and EXEC guard. The GDS form raises `Unsupported`. Source: RDNA2 ISA §10.4 and §12.13 (opcodes 176/177), LLVM GFX1030 assembly syntax. `tests/semantic/test_lds_addtid.py` compares the two forms with ordinary LDS reads/writes over two waves, including partial EXEC and emulated wave64. |
